diff --git a/PJLAB-LOCK-FIX.md b/PJLAB-LOCK-FIX.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..dceadd6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/PJLAB-LOCK-FIX.md
@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+# Mutter 46 Wayland modifier-state backport
+
+The native seat and Wayland keyboard in Mutter 46 independently track XKB
+locks. If the input method consumes a Caps Lock key event, the seat toggles
+but the Wayland event path misses it. Ordinary key events preserve the offset.
+This is a compositor consistency problem; a particular IME can trigger it.
+
+Backported from GNOME/mutter commits:
+- aeac0f33c2d06ac356af253ed52c175b48999f92: publish all native modifier state.
+- 1940c6eba938a9413b93694573501153788d1f0f: abstract keymap state getter.
+- 7d0d128e005fb737df7a8eea5e472d6c229898e1: remove duplicate Wayland accounting.
+  https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/mutter/-/merge_requests/4546
+
+Adaptations retain the Mutter 46 MetaAnonymousFile name and native keymap
+layout. Wayland publishes the backend modifier tuple rather than deriving
+locks from the subset of events that survive filtering. The XKB state is
+retained only for pressed-key bookkeeping, as in upstream.
+
+Validation must reproduce the consumed-Caps failure before the change and
+run the same test after it, including actual text input, IME switching,
+reconnection, and session restart. Unit/build success alone is insufficient.
+
+Mutter 46 integration: the native keymap state-changed signal also drives
+Wayland modifier notifications. This covers IMEs consuming both Caps events,
+without waiting for the next unfiltered key or using a polling/reset daemon.
+
+Event ordering follow-up: backport 0828fd6169c17ca54633f76936f03ac496f6b01e
+(GNOME MR 4686). Every key is preceded by its own captured modifier state;
+the resulting backend state is sent after key delivery. Updates used solely
+for pressed-key bookkeeping do not send protocol events. This prevents
+XWayland from toggling a lock after already receiving its resulting state
+and prevents later queued modifiers from changing earlier key meanings.
